---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CompressImageToTarget from '@/components/tools/image-edit/CompressImageToTarget.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-image-to-50kb">
  <CompressImageToTarget client:load targetKb={50} inputLabel="Drop the photo that has to come in under 50 KB" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The most common form limit there is</h2>
    <p>
      The 50 KB limit is on job applications, university admissions, visa portals, bank KYC forms and
      exam registrations. A phone photo never passes without editing: a modern camera file is 3–8 MB,
      sixty to a hundred and sixty times too large. But 50 KB is enough for a well-cropped portrait to
      look good.
    </p>

    <h2>What 50 KB buys you</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Subject</th><th>Comfortable dimensions</th><th>Typical quality chosen</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Headshot, plain background</td><td>600 × 800 px</td><td>75–85</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Document or certificate scan</td><td>1000 px wide</td><td>70–80</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Simple product photo</td><td>800 px wide</td><td>70–80</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Busy outdoor scene</td><td>500 × 700 px</td><td>55–70</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Group photo with faces to stay recognisable</td><td>700 px wide, crop tightly</td><td>60–75</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>When the form says “between 20 KB and 50 KB”</h2>
    <p>
      Aim at 50, not the middle. Set the target to 50 KB and read the size in the result card. The
      search always returns the largest file that fits, which is also the sharpest. If the result is
      well under 20 KB (this happens with a small, plain source image), the original is too small. Use
      a larger original; do not try to make the file bigger.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading the search</h2>
    <p>
      Expand “Show the search” in the result to see the full working: each encode, its quality, and if
      the byte count fitted. The algorithm is: try 92, then do a binary search of the range and keep the
      best file that fits. Look at it the first time: it shows how fast size falls with quality for your
      image, and so if you have room to spare or are at the limit.
    </p>

    <h2>Dimensions are usually specified too</h2>
    <p>
      Forms that limit the file size almost always limit the pixel size too; 200 × 230 px is a classic
      pair with a 20–50 KB limit. Put the required width into the width field before you press
      compress. The resize happens first, and the quality search then works inside the dimensions the
      form wants, so the file passes both the byte check and the pixel check.
    </p>

    <h2>Signature scans</h2>
    <p>
      A signature is mostly white with a thin dark line, which is very easy to compress. Scan or
      photograph it on plain white paper in even light, and consider PNG output: a clean signature can
      go under 20 KB losslessly, without the grey blur that JPEG puts around thin strokes.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing is uploaded, so retrying is free</h2>
    <p>
      Each encode runs on your CPU. The photo of your face and your ID documents stay on the device, and
      you can try a different crop, format or target as often as you like, with no queue, daily quota
      or account.
    </p>

    <h2>Other targets</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/compress-image-to-20kb">20 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/compress-image-to-100kb">100 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/compress-image-to-200kb">200 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/reduce-image-size-in-kb">any target you type</a>. For document photos, see also
      <a href="/passport-photo-maker">Passport Photo Maker</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
